/// Lesson sections for the info page — never shown on the practice screen.
const List<TechniqueSection> grooveLinearLesson = [
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why it matters",
    body:
        "A linear groove never stacks two sounds: hi-hat, snare and kick each get their own sixteenths. The result is transparent and busy at once, with every note audible, and it is the heart of the funk drumming of the 70s. It also trains you to place every limb exactly, because nothing hides behind a unison.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How to play it",
    body:
        "Line 1: kick on 1 and the \"a\" of 2, backbeat on 2 and 4, ghost notes on the \"and\" of 1 and of 3, and the right hand on the hi-hat on every sixteenth that is left. The hi-hat does not play with the kick or the snare; the left hand plays only the snare.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Practice tips",
    body:
        "Count sixteenths out loud (\"1 e and a\") and play line 1 at 60 until it flows. Lines 2 and 3 move the kick (\"and\" of 2, doubles on \"1 e\"), line 4 leaves only one backbeat, on 4, line 5 puts a ghost right after each backbeat. Line 6 is two different bars, line 7 keeps the backbeat on 2 only and surrounds the kicks with ghosts, line 8 accents the hi-hat between the beats in its first bar (kick on 1, the \"a\" of 2 and the \"a\" of 3) and ends on an open hi-hat on the \"a\" of 4; close it with the foot on the next one. Ghosts stay barely audible, backbeats loud.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Where you hear it",
    body:
        "David Garibaldi with Tower of Power (\"Oakland Stroke\", \"What Is Hip?\"), Steve Gadd's sticking-based grooves such as \"50 Ways to Leave Your Lover\", and funk and fusion drumming ever since.",
  ),
];
